\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Multi-VLM replication on the identical V4b crops, prompt, and matcher. Both models fabricate a device on a majority of clean control crops.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:multivlm}{{2}{6}{}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {IV-A}}Naming Succeeds on the Synthetic Confound and Fails on the Real One}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {IV-B}}The Failure Is a Property of General VLMs, Not of One Model}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The naming boundary: control-hallucination (device named on a confound-free crop) for each VLM on the synthetic confound versus the real chest tube. The boundary is {\fontencoding  {T1}\fontseries  {b}\selectfont  per-model}, not a flat clean split: Llama-4-Scout rises from {\fontencoding  {T1}\fontseries  {b}\selectfont  0.02} (synthetic) to {\fontencoding  {T1}\fontseries  {b}\selectfont  0.55} (real); Pixtral-12B rises from {\fontencoding  {T1}\fontseries  {b}\selectfont  0.31} (synthetic) to {\fontencoding  {T1}\fontseries  {b}\selectfont  0.83} (real). Both fabricate a device on a majority of clean control crops in the real-device setting; Pixtral already fabricates on nearly a third of clean synthetic-setting crops. The $<$10\% gate is cleared only by Scout on the synthetic confound.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:naming_boundary}{{2}{7}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {IV-C}}What Survives the Naming Failure}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Mitigation across 3 seeds (mean $\pm $ std). Worst-group and in-distribution (ID) accuracy and the reliance gap, for the real tube and the synthetic tag.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:mitigation}{{3}{7}{}{table.3}{}}
\citation{zech2018}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Mitigation comparison (Table~\ref {tab:mitigation}): worst-group and in-distribution accuracy for ERM, JTT, and DFR on the real tube confound and the synthetic tag confound, 3-seed mean $\pm $ std. On the tube, DFR and JTT both clear the ERM worst-group floor by roughly 12 points while preserving in-distribution accuracy; on the tag, the same procedures collapse in-distribution accuracy alongside the reliance gap, the cautionary result discussed in \S  \ref {sec:supporting}.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mitigationbar}{{3}{8}{}{figure.3}{}}
